		coreSummary: 'The Spanish subjunctive is a grammatical mood, not a tense: it doesn\'t mark when something happens, only whether the speaker presents it as fact versus wanted, doubted, ordered, or felt. Per the Real Academia Española, it appears in subordinate clauses after verbs of desire, influence, doubt, and emotion whenever the clause\'s subject differs from the main clause\'s — and it also fills in for the future tense inside temporal clauses like cuando, which Spanish grammar blocks from taking the indicative future or conditional.',
		language: 'Spanish',
