					'The formation rule is mechanical once you see the pattern: take the *yo* form of the present indicative, drop the final *-o*, and add the "opposite" set of endings. Verbs ending in *-ar* switch to *-e*-based endings: *hablar* → *hablo* → *habl-* → *hable, hables, hable, hablemos, habléis, hablen*. Verbs ending in *-er* or *-ir* switch to *-a*-based endings instead: *comer* → *como* → *com-* → *coma, comas, coma, comamos, comáis, coman*; *vivir* → *vivo* → *viv-* → *viva, vivas, viva...*.',
					'Because the stem is lifted straight from the *yo* form, any irregularity already baked into that form carries straight over into every person of the subjunctive, not just *yo*: *tener* → *tengo* → *tenga, tengas, tenga...*; *poner* → *pongo* → *ponga, pongas...*; *conocer* → *conozco* → *conozca, conozcas...*. If you already know a verb\'s irregular *yo* form in the present indicative, you already know its subjunctive stem.',
					'Spelling-preserving changes apply in every person of the subjunctive, which is a real difference from the present indicative (where they only show up in *yo*): *-car* verbs swap to *-que* (*buscar* → *busque*), *-gar* verbs swap to *-gue* (*llegar* → *llegue*), and *-zar* verbs swap to *-ce* (*empezar* → *empiece*). These exist purely to keep the consonant sound intact — the same logic Spanish spelling uses everywhere else, not a subjunctive-specific quirk.',
					'Stem-changing verbs split into two behaviors. *-ar* and *-er* "boot" verbs change in every person except *nosotros* and *vosotros*, mirroring the present indicative\'s own pattern: *pensar* → *piense*, but *pensemos*; *poder* → *pueda*, but *podamos*. *-ir* stem-changers go one step further and pick up a second, smaller vowel change specifically in *nosotros*/*vosotros*: *dormir* → *duerma* everywhere else, but *durmamos* (o→u, not o→ue); *pedir* → *pida* everywhere else, but *pidamos* (e→i).',
