		coreSummary: 'The Spanish condicional simple (cantaría) is classified by the Real Academia Española as a tense within the indicative mood, despite a long-standing debate over whether it belongs there or counts as a mood of its own. It marks a state of affairs as non-current — either hypothetical (si tuviera dinero, viajaría) or a future event reported from a past vantage point (anunció que se jubilaría) — and it also has a courtesy use (querría, podría) that RAE describes as more attenuated than the imperfect on the same politeness scale (quiero → quería → querría). English "would" is a false-friend guide here: its habitual-past sense ("she would visit every summer") maps to the Spanish imperfect, never the conditional.',
		language: 'Spanish',
